WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.823
Tokens, cookies, API keys, and credentials are not ordinary strings.

2
00:00:04.823 --> 00:00:11.489
In many systems, possession of a bearer value can grant access until it expires or is revoked.

3
00:00:11.489 --> 00:00:17.234
That means storage, transport, logging, display, and support handling all matter.

4
00:00:17.234 --> 00:00:28.723
Avoid placing tokens or credentials in URLs, query strings, browser storage, logs, screenshots, analytics, crash reports, copied links, or frontend configuration.

5
00:00:28.723 --> 00:00:37.233
These locations are often retained, synchronized, shared, or sent to systems that were not intended to hold credentials.

6
00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:46.027
Cookies need deliberate review of Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite, domain, path, lifetime, sliding expiration, and CSRF behavior.

7
00:00:46.027 --> 00:00:51.417
These settings should match the application architecture and user workflows.

8
00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:57.090
A cookie that is convenient for a demo may be too broadly scoped for production.

9
00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:01.346
A valid token or API key does not authorize every operation.

10
00:01:01.346 --> 00:01:11.204
The server still needs to check identity, scope, tenant, resource ownership, subscription, role, policy, and workflow state as appropriate.

11
00:01:11.204 --> 00:01:18.579
Authentication material proves something about the caller; authorization decides what the caller may do.

12
00:01:18.579 --> 00:01:23.473
Credential lifecycle should be designed before there is an emergency.

13
00:01:23.473 --> 00:01:36.522
Token lifetimes, refresh behavior, logout, revocation, rotation, key rollover, monitoring, and response procedures all affect how quickly exposed or stale credentials can be contained.

14
00:01:36.522 --> 00:01:41.699
Avoid confusing CORS, authentication, authorization, and CSRF protection.

15
00:01:41.699 --> 00:01:43.827
They solve different problems.

16
00:01:43.827 --> 00:01:49.358
A system can have a restrictive CORS policy and still have weak authorization.

17
00:01:49.358 --> 00:01:56.876
It can have strong authentication and still need CSRF protection for browser-based state-changing actions.
